WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.870 --> 00:00:07.580
TINO TERESHKO:
Welcome to GA 300,

00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:10.760
awesome new features to
help you manage BigQuery.

00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:13.010
My name is Tino Tereshko,
and I'm a product manager

00:00:13.010 --> 00:00:15.000
on Google BigQuery.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:18.590
So BigQuery, historically,
has been a serverless data

00:00:18.590 --> 00:00:21.410
warehouse, which kind
of means a lot of things

00:00:21.410 --> 00:00:22.590
to a lot of folks.

00:00:22.590 --> 00:00:24.320
But in practicality
what it means to

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:26.690
you is that you get
unlimited scale.

00:00:26.690 --> 00:00:28.410
BigQuery is very easy to use.

00:00:28.410 --> 00:00:30.710
It is automatically
self-optimizing.

00:00:30.710 --> 00:00:33.410
There's no virtual
machine-related performance

00:00:33.410 --> 00:00:36.080
cliffs of unexpected behaviors.

00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:38.600
It's got a high
level of reliability

00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:39.830
and a lot of other features.

00:00:39.830 --> 00:00:41.330
And of course, we
recently announced

00:00:41.330 --> 00:00:44.780
that we now have an
industry-best four nines

00:00:44.780 --> 00:00:46.490
of availability in our SLA.

00:00:46.490 --> 00:00:48.830
So all of these are benefits
of serverless that you

00:00:48.830 --> 00:00:51.050
get as a BigQuery customer.

00:00:51.050 --> 00:00:57.370
Now, folks who are on the far
end of the maturity spectrum--

00:00:57.370 --> 00:01:00.470
at large enterprises,
multinational organizations--

00:01:00.470 --> 00:01:03.235
they actually do want more
control, more predictability.

00:01:03.235 --> 00:01:04.610
So over the years
we've built out

00:01:04.610 --> 00:01:07.160
a number of features
and functionalities

00:01:07.160 --> 00:01:10.910
that enable those organizations
to take control of BigQuery

00:01:10.910 --> 00:01:14.790
while kind of getting the best
of BigQuery being serverless.

00:01:14.790 --> 00:01:19.220
In fact, we kind of built
BigQuery to be for everyone.

00:01:19.220 --> 00:01:21.870
Whether you are a startup,
whether you are a student,

00:01:21.870 --> 00:01:24.800
you're just kind of
experimenting in your garage,

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:26.830
we have functionality for that.

00:01:26.830 --> 00:01:29.400
Where you care about
simplicity, ease of use,

00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:31.370
you want to experiment quickly.

00:01:31.370 --> 00:01:34.610
And then, as you're growing
and you have larger scales,

00:01:34.610 --> 00:01:36.298
you want more flexibility.

00:01:36.298 --> 00:01:37.340
You want more efficiency.

00:01:37.340 --> 00:01:38.840
You want more predictability.

00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:40.440
We give you that functionality.

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:43.160
And again, all the way out
to the largest enterprises,

00:01:43.160 --> 00:01:46.520
where you have potentially
exabyte-scale use cases,

00:01:46.520 --> 00:01:47.840
you want control.

00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:49.067
You want predictability.

00:01:49.067 --> 00:01:49.900
You want monitoring.

00:01:49.900 --> 00:01:51.080
You want administration.

00:01:51.080 --> 00:01:54.890
So we are there to provide
you functionality wherever

00:01:54.890 --> 00:01:56.360
you are on this gradient.

00:01:56.360 --> 00:01:59.060
And actually, if you move
from the startup to growth

00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:00.823
to large enterprises,
you can take

00:02:00.823 --> 00:02:02.490
advantage of the
various functionalities

00:02:02.490 --> 00:02:04.070
that we have here.

00:02:04.070 --> 00:02:04.580
Brilliant.

00:02:04.580 --> 00:02:09.199
So let me illustrate
this whole concept

00:02:09.199 --> 00:02:11.200
through a fictional company.

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:14.600
And so, why not spruce up
the administration topic

00:02:14.600 --> 00:02:16.500
with a little bit of cats.

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:18.810
So this is actually
my cat, Rascal.

00:02:18.810 --> 00:02:20.240
He's just like any other cat.

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:21.950
He's got a lot of personality.

00:02:21.950 --> 00:02:24.800
And his interests
really include naps.

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:27.080
He really likes being petted.

00:02:27.080 --> 00:02:29.610
He stalks the
neighborhood quite a bit.

00:02:29.610 --> 00:02:32.390
So that's his primary things
in life that he cares about.

00:02:32.390 --> 00:02:37.640
So what if we used cutting-edge
technology to really figure out

00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:41.270
what Rascal does all
day and what he thinks

00:02:41.270 --> 00:02:43.640
and what drives him to
provide a better user

00:02:43.640 --> 00:02:45.500
experience for Rascal.

00:02:45.500 --> 00:02:48.140
So with that in mind,
we thought about

00:02:48.140 --> 00:02:52.160
critical feline requirements--
so short for CFRs, really.

00:02:52.160 --> 00:02:54.380
So, the first topic,
of course, is naps.

00:02:54.380 --> 00:02:57.110
So what do we need to
know about Rascal's naps?

00:02:57.110 --> 00:02:58.580
Well, we need to
know how often he

00:02:58.580 --> 00:03:01.280
naps, where he
naps-- locations--

00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:03.410
and actually total
nap time, which tends

00:03:03.410 --> 00:03:07.160
to be about 20 hours a day.

00:03:07.160 --> 00:03:10.070
The second one is Rascal
loves being petted.

00:03:10.070 --> 00:03:13.280
But there's a certain
threshold to petting.

00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:16.370
So we need to know, what is
the optimal number of pettings

00:03:16.370 --> 00:03:18.470
that he can sustain?

00:03:18.470 --> 00:03:19.970
Does he like to be
pet on the belly?

00:03:19.970 --> 00:03:21.428
Does he like to be
pet on the head?

00:03:21.428 --> 00:03:23.030
That's very critical
to understand.

00:03:23.030 --> 00:03:25.280
And finally, for
our sakes, we need

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:29.240
to know how likely is Rascal
to be upset if we give him

00:03:29.240 --> 00:03:30.360
just one more pet.

00:03:30.360 --> 00:03:32.000
That's really important to know.

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:33.740
And finally, the third
interest of Rascal

00:03:33.740 --> 00:03:35.300
is, he's stalking
the neighborhood.

00:03:35.300 --> 00:03:37.330
So we need to know the
perimeter of his patrol.

00:03:37.330 --> 00:03:39.350
What is his territory?

00:03:39.350 --> 00:03:41.660
And a different cat
right down the street

00:03:41.660 --> 00:03:43.490
has a different territory.

00:03:43.490 --> 00:03:45.570
We need to know how
often he patrols it.

00:03:45.570 --> 00:03:50.090
And maybe we can even map
the social network of Rascal

00:03:50.090 --> 00:03:51.977
and how he interacts
with all the other cats.

00:03:51.977 --> 00:03:53.810
These are all really,
really critical things

00:03:53.810 --> 00:03:55.640
to know about felines.

00:03:55.640 --> 00:03:59.630
So to that extent, we're
introducing 23andMeow.

00:03:59.630 --> 00:04:01.700
This is a brand new,
innovative startup.

00:04:01.700 --> 00:04:03.730
It's internet of cats.

00:04:03.730 --> 00:04:08.540
What 23andMeow does is it
provides smart, connected

00:04:08.540 --> 00:04:09.800
collars for cats.

00:04:09.800 --> 00:04:11.220
Let me explain.

00:04:11.220 --> 00:04:14.330
So you put this
collar on your cat,

00:04:14.330 --> 00:04:18.950
and the smart collar has
lots of different sensors

00:04:18.950 --> 00:04:22.110
that collect telemetry
data in real time.

00:04:22.110 --> 00:04:24.880
So there is a
geospatial capability,

00:04:24.880 --> 00:04:26.570
so it gets location of Rascal.

00:04:26.570 --> 00:04:28.135
There's a temperature sensor.

00:04:28.135 --> 00:04:29.510
There's an
accelerator meter that

00:04:29.510 --> 00:04:31.385
will tell you what's
the velocity of your cat

00:04:31.385 --> 00:04:34.010
traveling and the acceleration.

00:04:34.010 --> 00:04:37.040
There is also a
microphone and a camera.

00:04:37.040 --> 00:04:40.170
So you can identify sounds and
even take video and pictures.

00:04:40.170 --> 00:04:43.310
So all of that data gets
captured and collected

00:04:43.310 --> 00:04:46.250
in real time, and then we
perform predictive real time

00:04:46.250 --> 00:04:48.330
analytics on all
that information.

00:04:48.330 --> 00:04:49.970
And so, in this
sense, we give you

00:04:49.970 --> 00:04:52.550
a better picture of what
your cat does all day

00:04:52.550 --> 00:04:54.820
and what are your
cat's preferences.

00:04:54.820 --> 00:04:56.090
It's beautiful.

00:04:56.090 --> 00:04:59.120
So let's take a look
at the tech stack.

00:04:59.120 --> 00:05:03.320
So 23andMeow heavily
relies on BigQuery.

00:05:03.320 --> 00:05:05.450
In fact, it's essentially
the only thing

00:05:05.450 --> 00:05:07.230
that 23andMeow is doing.

00:05:07.230 --> 00:05:11.932
It picked BigQuery because
it's very easy to get started.

00:05:11.932 --> 00:05:13.640
Time to market is
critical, because there

00:05:13.640 --> 00:05:15.098
are other similar
startups that are

00:05:15.098 --> 00:05:16.940
trying to do the same thing.

00:05:16.940 --> 00:05:18.730
So 23andMeow wanted
to pick technology

00:05:18.730 --> 00:05:21.500
that's serverless, that's
easy to get going with,

00:05:21.500 --> 00:05:23.660
and also easy to grow with.

00:05:23.660 --> 00:05:25.770
So let's walk through
this technology stack.

00:05:25.770 --> 00:05:27.290
So first of all,
we already talked

00:05:27.290 --> 00:05:30.440
about how a number of
sensors are sending data

00:05:30.440 --> 00:05:32.460
into BigQuery in real time.

00:05:32.460 --> 00:05:34.670
That includes
geospatial sensors.

00:05:34.670 --> 00:05:37.580
And so geospatial data
comes into BigQuery.

00:05:37.580 --> 00:05:40.730
Point number two, this data
is ingested in real time

00:05:40.730 --> 00:05:44.690
using BigQuery's real time APIs
to get that data into BigQuery

00:05:44.690 --> 00:05:46.760
for immediate access.

00:05:46.760 --> 00:05:48.980
Number three, BigQuery's
new integration

00:05:48.980 --> 00:05:51.560
with AutoML and
the Cloud AI stack

00:05:51.560 --> 00:05:55.312
enables us to perform
machine learning right inside

00:05:55.312 --> 00:05:56.770
of the data warehouse,
right inside

00:05:56.770 --> 00:05:59.190
of BigQuery, on
this real time data.

00:05:59.190 --> 00:06:00.980
So we've developed
cat behavior models

00:06:00.980 --> 00:06:04.070
like danger of
incremental pettings.

00:06:04.070 --> 00:06:06.700
How about the favorite
body part to be petted on?

00:06:06.700 --> 00:06:08.470
And actually favorite
nap-time locations.

00:06:08.470 --> 00:06:11.180
These are all really advanced
machine learning technologies

00:06:11.180 --> 00:06:14.450
that we've implemented here
to develop this functionality.

00:06:14.450 --> 00:06:16.800
And number four, we have
a number of public data

00:06:16.800 --> 00:06:20.390
sets that we include that
we enrich our data with.

00:06:20.390 --> 00:06:23.540
So for example, weather, that
depends what your cat does,

00:06:23.540 --> 00:06:25.520
depending what the
weather outside is.

00:06:25.520 --> 00:06:27.290
And then we're using
materialized views,

00:06:27.290 --> 00:06:29.632
and we're moving the
data into BI engine.

00:06:29.632 --> 00:06:30.590
And that's number five.

00:06:30.590 --> 00:06:33.440
And of course, new, upcoming
BI engine enhancements

00:06:33.440 --> 00:06:37.250
allow you to run BigQuery
SQL on top of BI engine.

00:06:37.250 --> 00:06:40.700
And number six, Locker, that
recently joined our family,

00:06:40.700 --> 00:06:42.878
is used for dashboarding
of this data.

00:06:42.878 --> 00:06:44.670
So this is an entire
stack that essentially

00:06:44.670 --> 00:06:47.560
lives on top of BigQuery.

00:06:47.560 --> 00:06:50.450
Now, if you take a look at
those things, one through six,

00:06:50.450 --> 00:06:53.270
all those things are
essentially unique to BigQuery,

00:06:53.270 --> 00:06:54.950
or almost unique.

00:06:54.950 --> 00:06:57.043
Which means that you
don't need to build

00:06:57.043 --> 00:06:58.460
a number of different
technologies

00:06:58.460 --> 00:07:00.400
to take advantage
of all these things.

00:07:00.400 --> 00:07:03.920
This is all integrated
right into BigQuery for you.

00:07:03.920 --> 00:07:05.660
It's beautiful.

00:07:05.660 --> 00:07:07.040
So we started out small, right?

00:07:07.040 --> 00:07:08.330
We rolled out our startup.

00:07:08.330 --> 00:07:10.860
We have a few trusted
cats that we're using.

00:07:10.860 --> 00:07:13.370
And so we're just using
BigQuery's perpetual free tier.

00:07:13.370 --> 00:07:16.280
If you're not familiar, you
get 10 gigabytes of storage

00:07:16.280 --> 00:07:18.330
for free every single month.

00:07:18.330 --> 00:07:21.530
And you get a terabyte of data
processed every single month.

00:07:21.530 --> 00:07:23.790
And we do this perpetually,
no strings attached.

00:07:23.790 --> 00:07:27.590
You can keep using the free
tier for as long as you want.

00:07:27.590 --> 00:07:30.230
But now that we've kind
of expanded our offering

00:07:30.230 --> 00:07:34.370
beyond the trusted cats,
now we have 1,000 cats

00:07:34.370 --> 00:07:36.380
that are using our collar.

00:07:36.380 --> 00:07:38.630
So we're growing with BigQuery.

00:07:38.630 --> 00:07:40.190
We're on that gradient.

00:07:40.190 --> 00:07:43.040
And so we're using BigQuery's
on-demand bytes processed

00:07:43.040 --> 00:07:43.940
model, right?

00:07:43.940 --> 00:07:47.060
This is a billing model
that is purely consumption.

00:07:47.060 --> 00:07:49.130
You're only paying
for what you consume.

00:07:49.130 --> 00:07:50.900
You pay per query.

00:07:50.900 --> 00:07:53.090
So there's no wastage,
it's super efficient,

00:07:53.090 --> 00:07:54.860
and there's zero
administrative overhead.

00:07:54.860 --> 00:07:56.485
You don't have to
think about anything.

00:07:56.485 --> 00:07:58.050
There's nothing to set up.

00:07:58.050 --> 00:08:00.390
But if you want to kind
of manage your cost

00:08:00.390 --> 00:08:03.728
and make sure there's no runaway
bills or anything like that,

00:08:03.728 --> 00:08:05.020
we're leveraging cost controls.

00:08:07.970 --> 00:08:10.270
So now we've thought
about, how do we

00:08:10.270 --> 00:08:12.550
expand our offer to make
things more interesting.

00:08:12.550 --> 00:08:16.200
So we introduced an additional
sensor to our cat collars

00:08:16.200 --> 00:08:18.460
that actually measures
cat pheromones

00:08:18.460 --> 00:08:21.460
and actually smells what
the cat smells like.

00:08:21.460 --> 00:08:25.210
And it tracks the cat pheromone
excretions and then runs

00:08:25.210 --> 00:08:27.130
machine learning
models to understand

00:08:27.130 --> 00:08:29.960
what your cat is feeling
at that particular moment.

00:08:29.960 --> 00:08:31.890
So this is called
the Emotions API,

00:08:31.890 --> 00:08:36.409
and it's available to all of
our 23andMeow customers today.

00:08:36.409 --> 00:08:38.650
So on the back of
the Emotions API,

00:08:38.650 --> 00:08:41.419
23andMeow has become
really successful.

00:08:41.419 --> 00:08:45.220
We now have a whole 1
million daily active cats.

00:08:45.220 --> 00:08:46.720
Phenomenal success.

00:08:46.720 --> 00:08:48.720
But now, actually, our
infrastructure costs

00:08:48.720 --> 00:08:50.570
have started to
grow as well, right?

00:08:50.570 --> 00:08:52.000
We're running more analytics.

00:08:52.000 --> 00:08:54.220
We're using more CPU and RAM.

00:08:54.220 --> 00:08:55.990
We have more dashboards.

00:08:55.990 --> 00:08:58.910
So our leverage of
BigQuery is growing.

00:08:58.910 --> 00:09:02.380
And so we need to start worrying
about maybe monitoring it,

00:09:02.380 --> 00:09:04.670
maybe administering
it a little bit more.

00:09:04.670 --> 00:09:06.610
And so the question
pops up very typically

00:09:06.610 --> 00:09:10.810
with BigQuery customers
is, are slots right for me?

00:09:10.810 --> 00:09:14.280
For the uninitiated, there's
two billing models on BigQuery.

00:09:14.280 --> 00:09:16.780
There's the base process model
that most customers start out

00:09:16.780 --> 00:09:17.540
with.

00:09:17.540 --> 00:09:19.230
And then there's
the slots model.

00:09:19.230 --> 00:09:21.230
And the slots model is optional.

00:09:21.230 --> 00:09:23.140
It tends to be for
the larger customers,

00:09:23.140 --> 00:09:25.240
and it has some
really nice benefits.

00:09:25.240 --> 00:09:28.610
So the question is, are the
BigQuery slots right for me?

00:09:28.610 --> 00:09:32.080
Well, in order to help
our customers figure out

00:09:32.080 --> 00:09:34.150
if that is the
right option, we're

00:09:34.150 --> 00:09:36.880
launching something called
BigQuery slot recommendations.

00:09:36.880 --> 00:09:39.050
It's currently in Alpha.

00:09:39.050 --> 00:09:41.650
So this is advanced
machine learning logic

00:09:41.650 --> 00:09:45.280
that we will run
on your usage data,

00:09:45.280 --> 00:09:47.890
of course, using all the
right privacy controls.

00:09:47.890 --> 00:09:51.090
And we will tell you proactively
whether the slots model

00:09:51.090 --> 00:09:53.650
is the right model for
you, without sacrificing

00:09:53.650 --> 00:09:54.430
performance.

00:09:54.430 --> 00:09:56.200
So you could
potentially save money

00:09:56.200 --> 00:09:59.360
without sacrificing
performance, which is fantastic.

00:09:59.360 --> 00:10:01.620
And of course, the other
pricing recommendation

00:10:01.620 --> 00:10:03.750
that we launched kind
of helps you figure out

00:10:03.750 --> 00:10:07.570
whether you need to use
monthly slots or annual slots.

00:10:07.570 --> 00:10:08.710
Go use that today.

00:10:08.710 --> 00:10:10.210
And there's a related
session called

00:10:10.210 --> 00:10:14.392
CMT 100 that talks about that
in a little bit of a greater

00:10:14.392 --> 00:10:15.910
depth.

00:10:15.910 --> 00:10:17.850
So what are BigQuery slots?

00:10:17.850 --> 00:10:20.180
It's a very frequent
question that I hear.

00:10:20.180 --> 00:10:23.770
Well, simply, a slot is a CPU.

00:10:23.770 --> 00:10:25.880
You can think
about it like that.

00:10:25.880 --> 00:10:30.010
And a CPU processes your
queries, runs analytics.

00:10:30.010 --> 00:10:33.820
So whenever you purchase a
slot, or a number of slots,

00:10:33.820 --> 00:10:36.630
you purchase analytic
processing compute capability.

00:10:36.630 --> 00:10:39.220
You purchased
throughput, which means

00:10:39.220 --> 00:10:42.880
that the number of slots that
you have will either give you

00:10:42.880 --> 00:10:46.030
faster queries or will give
you more queries to run

00:10:46.030 --> 00:10:47.810
at the same time, or both.

00:10:47.810 --> 00:10:50.280
You're kind of buying
a cluster of BigQuery

00:10:50.280 --> 00:10:52.090
with BigQuery slots.

00:10:52.090 --> 00:10:57.760
And you can buy slots today
with a per second commitment,

00:10:57.760 --> 00:10:59.852
per month, or per year.

00:10:59.852 --> 00:11:01.810
The per second commitment
is called flex slots.

00:11:01.810 --> 00:11:03.760
And there's no difference
between those slots,

00:11:03.760 --> 00:11:07.460
except for flex slots you can
cancel just after 60 seconds.

00:11:07.460 --> 00:11:10.793
So you can use them whenever
and cancel them whenever.

00:11:10.793 --> 00:11:12.460
But they're a little
bit more expensive.

00:11:12.460 --> 00:11:15.090
Monthly slots, you commit
to an entire month.

00:11:15.090 --> 00:11:20.002
So you can't cancel
them or downsize them

00:11:20.002 --> 00:11:22.210
for the duration of a month,
but you get a little bit

00:11:22.210 --> 00:11:23.290
of a discount on that.

00:11:23.290 --> 00:11:26.710
And then annual is, of
course, the cheapest option,

00:11:26.710 --> 00:11:28.150
but the least flexible.

00:11:28.150 --> 00:11:30.730
You commit to a full
year for that capacity,

00:11:30.730 --> 00:11:33.390
but you get a discount.

00:11:33.390 --> 00:11:36.220
So how are queries using slots?

00:11:36.220 --> 00:11:37.290
Let's talk about that.

00:11:37.290 --> 00:11:41.590
So a query, really, inside of
BigQuery is the Dynamic DAG--

00:11:41.590 --> 00:11:43.140
Directed Acyclic Graph.

00:11:43.140 --> 00:11:45.992
What that means is it's dynamic.

00:11:45.992 --> 00:11:46.950
What does dynamic mean?

00:11:46.950 --> 00:11:51.020
Dynamic means that, as
the query progresses,

00:11:51.020 --> 00:11:53.820
our query execution
plan changes.

00:11:53.820 --> 00:11:56.130
We learn more about what
you're trying to do,

00:11:56.130 --> 00:11:58.140
we learn more about
the data patterns,

00:11:58.140 --> 00:12:01.380
and we decide on the fly
the best path of execution.

00:12:01.380 --> 00:12:03.630
This is really, really
powerful and really accelerates

00:12:03.630 --> 00:12:05.910
queries for you.

00:12:05.910 --> 00:12:07.830
But what does the DAG part mean?

00:12:07.830 --> 00:12:10.110
The DAG part means
that a BigQuery query

00:12:10.110 --> 00:12:12.480
is a series of stages.

00:12:12.480 --> 00:12:13.770
Now, they can run in parallel.

00:12:13.770 --> 00:12:15.120
They can run serially.

00:12:15.120 --> 00:12:19.210
There's all kinds of patterns
that we can leverage there.

00:12:19.210 --> 00:12:22.530
And each stage is
essentially a tiny mini-query

00:12:22.530 --> 00:12:24.290
in its own right.

00:12:24.290 --> 00:12:26.880
And each stage has a
parallelization factor,

00:12:26.880 --> 00:12:28.200
anywhere from one to--

00:12:28.200 --> 00:12:29.610
I've seen billions.

00:12:29.610 --> 00:12:31.650
Which basically
BigQuery says, I can

00:12:31.650 --> 00:12:34.200
parallelize this particular
stage of the query

00:12:34.200 --> 00:12:36.760
by so-and-so amount.

00:12:36.760 --> 00:12:39.450
And it's very typical to
have this parallelization

00:12:39.450 --> 00:12:41.880
factor in thousands.

00:12:41.880 --> 00:12:44.890
And a slot is,
essentially, that CPU

00:12:44.890 --> 00:12:47.250
that executes a
single parallelization

00:12:47.250 --> 00:12:49.690
unit of a single stage.

00:12:49.690 --> 00:12:51.510
So in this particular
picture, you

00:12:51.510 --> 00:12:54.630
see the first stage has 10,000--

00:12:54.630 --> 00:12:57.570
a parallelization
factor of 10,000.

00:12:57.570 --> 00:13:00.780
That means that
BigQuery could use up

00:13:00.780 --> 00:13:03.950
to 10,000 slots at
any point in time

00:13:03.950 --> 00:13:06.840
to execute this particular
stage of the query.

00:13:06.840 --> 00:13:10.860
But now, 10,000 slots
is a lot of slots.

00:13:10.860 --> 00:13:13.680
If you have a little bit
fewer than 10,000 slots,

00:13:13.680 --> 00:13:17.760
we will simply burn
through those slots that

00:13:17.760 --> 00:13:19.850
are not available with time.

00:13:19.850 --> 00:13:21.970
So let's say if
you have 500 slots.

00:13:21.970 --> 00:13:24.400
500 slots will
execute that stage,

00:13:24.400 --> 00:13:27.210
and then 9500 slots will
essentially queue up.

00:13:27.210 --> 00:13:29.940
And then, as the first
500 slots free up,

00:13:29.940 --> 00:13:32.580
they will burn through that
pent-up demand with time.

00:13:32.580 --> 00:13:35.880
And so think of a query
as a series of stages.

00:13:35.880 --> 00:13:38.670
And each stage consumes
any number from one

00:13:38.670 --> 00:13:40.860
to billions of
slots, potentially.

00:13:40.860 --> 00:13:43.920
And each stage doesn't
have to get all its slots.

00:13:43.920 --> 00:13:46.770
It will kind of wait for
the slots to come in.

00:13:46.770 --> 00:13:49.650
Hopefully I explained
that well for you.

00:13:49.650 --> 00:13:52.560
Now, when you have multiple
queries running concurrently,

00:13:52.560 --> 00:13:54.570
what happens in BigQuery?

00:13:54.570 --> 00:13:56.940
We use this concept
called fair scheduling.

00:13:56.940 --> 00:14:00.180
What that means is, each
query gets equal right

00:14:00.180 --> 00:14:02.940
to the available
provision slots.

00:14:02.940 --> 00:14:05.760
Now, if a small
query comes in, it

00:14:05.760 --> 00:14:07.740
doesn't have to use
its equal right.

00:14:07.740 --> 00:14:09.820
It potentially could
use only a single slot

00:14:09.820 --> 00:14:11.280
if it's a small query.

00:14:11.280 --> 00:14:13.020
Or on the opposite
end of the spectrum,

00:14:13.020 --> 00:14:15.540
a query could request
millions of slots.

00:14:15.540 --> 00:14:19.952
But they get the equal right to
the capacity that's available.

00:14:19.952 --> 00:14:21.660
Now, I've mentioned
this before already--

00:14:21.660 --> 00:14:25.110
if a query doesn't get
100% its request the slots,

00:14:25.110 --> 00:14:27.030
those slots just
kind of queue up.

00:14:27.030 --> 00:14:31.050
So, it will burn through
that pent-up capacity

00:14:31.050 --> 00:14:33.528
as slots become available.

00:14:33.528 --> 00:14:35.070
And the final thing
I want to mention

00:14:35.070 --> 00:14:38.730
is, BigQuery continuously
does this recalculation.

00:14:38.730 --> 00:14:39.980
This happens in real time.

00:14:39.980 --> 00:14:42.000
So whenever new
queries come in, we

00:14:42.000 --> 00:14:44.340
will redistribute slots in line.

00:14:44.340 --> 00:14:48.720
Now, the effect here is
queries speed up or slow down

00:14:48.720 --> 00:14:52.200
in real time, rather than
kind of cancel or anything

00:14:52.200 --> 00:14:53.040
like that.

00:14:53.040 --> 00:14:54.130
So let me give an example.

00:14:54.130 --> 00:14:55.505
So here on the
right side, you'll

00:14:55.505 --> 00:14:59.007
see that the environment
has 2,000 slots in total,

00:14:59.007 --> 00:15:00.840
and there are four
queries running in there.

00:15:00.840 --> 00:15:02.430
And they're fairly
large queries,

00:15:02.430 --> 00:15:06.880
so each one is
asking for 500 slots,

00:15:06.880 --> 00:15:08.790
for the maximum amount of slots.

00:15:08.790 --> 00:15:10.470
So our fair schedule
will actually

00:15:10.470 --> 00:15:13.550
give each query 500 slots.

00:15:13.550 --> 00:15:16.070
And let's say a fifth query
comes in that's a large query,

00:15:16.070 --> 00:15:18.250
and it needs a lot
of slots, as well.

00:15:18.250 --> 00:15:20.520
What we're going to do
automatically, in real time,

00:15:20.520 --> 00:15:23.700
is we're going to take 100
slots away from each of the four

00:15:23.700 --> 00:15:24.660
queries.

00:15:24.660 --> 00:15:26.440
We'll just pause that work--

00:15:26.440 --> 00:15:27.720
we're not going to waste it--

00:15:27.720 --> 00:15:30.030
and we will give it
to the fifth query.

00:15:30.030 --> 00:15:34.050
So now we have five queries
running with 400 slots each.

00:15:34.050 --> 00:15:36.210
What that did to the
original four queries,

00:15:36.210 --> 00:15:39.370
it just made them a little
bit slower than before.

00:15:39.370 --> 00:15:41.290
So the system works
really, really well.

00:15:41.290 --> 00:15:45.230
It actually biases against
large runaway queries.

00:15:45.230 --> 00:15:49.830
And it biases for smaller
queries that have lower latency

00:15:49.830 --> 00:15:50.800
requirements.

00:15:50.800 --> 00:15:54.600
So it's a very,
very useful pattern.

00:15:54.600 --> 00:15:56.310
So BigQuery slots pricing.

00:15:56.310 --> 00:15:57.810
Let's talk about
the benefits here.

00:15:57.810 --> 00:16:01.290
The first one, of
course, it's predictable.

00:16:01.290 --> 00:16:03.540
You control how much you spend.

00:16:03.540 --> 00:16:07.080
You explicitly tell us, I want
to purchase x amount of slots

00:16:07.080 --> 00:16:09.822
and it's going to cost me
a certain amount of money.

00:16:09.822 --> 00:16:11.280
Now, I've mentioned
before, we have

00:16:11.280 --> 00:16:12.405
three types of commitments.

00:16:12.405 --> 00:16:15.600
We have monthly,
annual, and flex slots.

00:16:15.600 --> 00:16:18.000
Monthly and annual
require you to commit

00:16:18.000 --> 00:16:19.782
to 30 days or a full year.

00:16:19.782 --> 00:16:21.990
And flex slots essentially
have no commitment period.

00:16:21.990 --> 00:16:24.910
So you can purchase them and
cancel them whenever you want.

00:16:24.910 --> 00:16:27.540
The other thing is, we
don't rely on t-shirt sizes.

00:16:27.540 --> 00:16:31.320
You can buy slots in a
number of granularities,

00:16:31.320 --> 00:16:33.970
and that's really,
really powerful.

00:16:33.970 --> 00:16:36.270
The second part I
should say is, a lot

00:16:36.270 --> 00:16:38.100
of features come with slots.

00:16:38.100 --> 00:16:39.833
We don't have a
special type of slots

00:16:39.833 --> 00:16:41.250
that you have to
purchase in order

00:16:41.250 --> 00:16:42.600
to get materialized views.

00:16:42.600 --> 00:16:44.100
We don't have a
special type of slot

00:16:44.100 --> 00:16:46.790
you need to purchase in order to
buy advanced security features

00:16:46.790 --> 00:16:49.710
or customer master encryption
keys-- like time travel,

00:16:49.710 --> 00:16:52.470
like DDoS protection
that we provide.

00:16:52.470 --> 00:16:54.780
All of that is
included in slots.

00:16:54.780 --> 00:16:57.360
We also do with things
like batch ingest, which

00:16:57.360 --> 00:16:59.740
actually doesn't consume
your slot capacity.

00:16:59.740 --> 00:17:02.010
It doesn't contend
with your queries.

00:17:02.010 --> 00:17:04.150
It comes from somewhere else.

00:17:04.150 --> 00:17:06.750
So you really don't pay
for batch ingest, at all.

00:17:06.750 --> 00:17:09.720
And we also do a lot of
background processes--

00:17:09.720 --> 00:17:11.640
like automatically
clustering-- that we

00:17:11.640 --> 00:17:13.410
don't charge for at all.

00:17:13.410 --> 00:17:16.137
So it's something that we
provide to you as a service

00:17:16.137 --> 00:17:18.720
to make sure that your queries
are running exceptionally well,

00:17:18.720 --> 00:17:20.980
and we don't charge you for it.

00:17:20.980 --> 00:17:24.180
And of course, the
industry-best four nines of SLA

00:17:24.180 --> 00:17:26.130
just comes with any slot.

00:17:26.130 --> 00:17:27.660
You don't have to
pay extra for it.

00:17:27.660 --> 00:17:30.330
I should also mention that
all of these properties you

00:17:30.330 --> 00:17:33.880
get with bytes processed
model, as well.

00:17:33.880 --> 00:17:35.760
And finally, because
we are serverless--

00:17:35.760 --> 00:17:38.700
because we're not bound
to virtual machines--

00:17:38.700 --> 00:17:42.570
we get to provide some really
interesting benefits to you.

00:17:42.570 --> 00:17:44.860
Our architecture is very
unique in that sense.

00:17:44.860 --> 00:17:47.400
So, the first one is, we
can provision capacity

00:17:47.400 --> 00:17:48.720
almost instantly.

00:17:48.720 --> 00:17:51.390
We don't have virtual machines
to spin up a provision for you.

00:17:51.390 --> 00:17:54.120
We don't need to move data
into your virtual machine

00:17:54.120 --> 00:17:55.890
to get performance.

00:17:55.890 --> 00:17:56.820
It's just serverless.

00:17:56.820 --> 00:17:58.120
It just lives there.

00:17:58.120 --> 00:18:00.947
So for us, scaling is
just a number change.

00:18:00.947 --> 00:18:03.030
The other thing is, of
course, the virtual machine

00:18:03.030 --> 00:18:04.580
doesn't have to be
a pre-warned, data

00:18:04.580 --> 00:18:06.760
doesn't need to be moved
into the virtual machine,

00:18:06.760 --> 00:18:09.480
so you get optimal
performance immediately.

00:18:09.480 --> 00:18:11.812
And the final bit that's a
little bit hard to capture,

00:18:11.812 --> 00:18:13.770
but it's really, really
important for real life

00:18:13.770 --> 00:18:14.730
use cases--

00:18:14.730 --> 00:18:17.130
whenever you
increase capacity, it

00:18:17.130 --> 00:18:20.380
impacts your queries
in flight immediately.

00:18:20.380 --> 00:18:22.260
So if you have a query
that's running inside

00:18:22.260 --> 00:18:24.990
of your environment and you
need to accelerate that query,

00:18:24.990 --> 00:18:27.910
adding more capacity
will do that immediately.

00:18:27.910 --> 00:18:30.300
You don't have to undo the
query, cancel the query,

00:18:30.300 --> 00:18:31.710
and start a new one.

00:18:31.710 --> 00:18:35.970
Queries in flight benefit
from capacity changes.

00:18:35.970 --> 00:18:37.200
Fantastic.

00:18:37.200 --> 00:18:40.090
One last comment on the
different commitment types.

00:18:40.090 --> 00:18:41.730
I want to make it
really, really clear.

00:18:41.730 --> 00:18:44.250
So, we have three commitment
types-- flex slots, monthly,

00:18:44.250 --> 00:18:45.360
and annual.

00:18:45.360 --> 00:18:47.100
So think about what
is the proper use

00:18:47.100 --> 00:18:49.380
case for how to combine these.

00:18:49.380 --> 00:18:52.040
You don't have to use
only one of those types.

00:18:52.040 --> 00:18:53.490
It's just slots, again.

00:18:53.490 --> 00:18:57.100
And the difference is the length
of the commitment and price.

00:18:57.100 --> 00:18:59.400
So you can think about
it workload by workload.

00:18:59.400 --> 00:19:02.760
You can have steady state
monthly or annual commitments

00:19:02.760 --> 00:19:06.720
there that are used 24/7.

00:19:06.720 --> 00:19:08.410
And you get a lower
price for them.

00:19:08.410 --> 00:19:10.560
And then you can use
flex slots to have

00:19:10.560 --> 00:19:14.850
kind of this first edition
capacity to the steady state

00:19:14.850 --> 00:19:18.060
that is used for seconds,
minutes, hours, or potentially

00:19:18.060 --> 00:19:18.780
days.

00:19:18.780 --> 00:19:21.030
So you can combine these
two, and that becomes really,

00:19:21.030 --> 00:19:23.440
really powerful.

00:19:23.440 --> 00:19:26.020
I'm really delighted to
announce that, today, we're

00:19:26.020 --> 00:19:28.180
offering 100 slots pricing.

00:19:28.180 --> 00:19:30.880
So before today,
you had to commit

00:19:30.880 --> 00:19:37.150
to 500 slots, which,
admittedly, is quite a lot

00:19:37.150 --> 00:19:38.710
for a number of our customers.

00:19:38.710 --> 00:19:43.280
So with a monthly contract,
500 slots was $10,000 a month.

00:19:43.280 --> 00:19:45.640
So a lot of our
customers really wanted

00:19:45.640 --> 00:19:48.520
to start using slots pricing,
but they wanted a lower price

00:19:48.520 --> 00:19:49.040
point.

00:19:49.040 --> 00:19:51.290
So we're introducing 100 slots.

00:19:51.290 --> 00:19:53.500
What that means is,
on a monthly contract,

00:19:53.500 --> 00:19:55.270
it's $2,000 per month.

00:19:55.270 --> 00:19:58.430
On an annual contract,
it's $1,700 per month.

00:19:58.430 --> 00:20:01.990
And on a flex contract,
it's just $4 per hour.

00:20:01.990 --> 00:20:05.860
So you could purchase 100 slots,
cancel them after one hour,

00:20:05.860 --> 00:20:07.840
and you only pay $4.

00:20:07.840 --> 00:20:09.790
So it's really
inexpensive, and I'm

00:20:09.790 --> 00:20:12.040
hoping that a lot of our
customers embrace this model.

00:20:15.640 --> 00:20:18.430
We're continuing to expand our
administration functionality

00:20:18.430 --> 00:20:19.640
in many different ways.

00:20:19.640 --> 00:20:22.060
So Admin Panel UI is
one of those investments

00:20:22.060 --> 00:20:22.940
that we're making.

00:20:22.940 --> 00:20:23.890
It's on the the
road map, and we're

00:20:23.890 --> 00:20:26.260
hoping to make it available
in the next few months.

00:20:26.260 --> 00:20:29.110
Essentially what the
Administration Panel UI does,

00:20:29.110 --> 00:20:32.680
it's a BigQuery native
monitoring and administration

00:20:32.680 --> 00:20:34.070
platform.

00:20:34.070 --> 00:20:36.250
It allows you to
chart how are you

00:20:36.250 --> 00:20:38.660
using BigQuery in real time.

00:20:38.660 --> 00:20:41.650
So it's going to allow you
to monitor slots, resources,

00:20:41.650 --> 00:20:46.270
queries, jobs, users,
and so on and so forth.

00:20:46.270 --> 00:20:49.810
You'll also be able to do this
in real time and historically--

00:20:49.810 --> 00:20:52.033
going back 30 days or more--

00:20:52.033 --> 00:20:53.950
which means that you can
zoom in and zoom out,

00:20:53.950 --> 00:20:55.270
depending on the use case.

00:20:55.270 --> 00:20:57.310
For the real time
use case, you can

00:20:57.310 --> 00:21:00.820
identify whether somebody
in your organization

00:21:00.820 --> 00:21:03.760
is using too much and
perform root cause analysis

00:21:03.760 --> 00:21:04.730
in that way.

00:21:04.730 --> 00:21:06.430
Whereas if you zoom
out historically,

00:21:06.430 --> 00:21:09.280
you'll be able to decide whether
you need more capacity or less

00:21:09.280 --> 00:21:13.060
capacity based on your
various usage patterns.

00:21:15.750 --> 00:21:18.200
The next set of features
that we've recently launched

00:21:18.200 --> 00:21:20.320
is information schema views.

00:21:20.320 --> 00:21:22.830
Now, a number of these
views are already available.

00:21:22.830 --> 00:21:24.650
Some of them are
general availability.

00:21:24.650 --> 00:21:27.480
And what these views allow
you to do is, in real time

00:21:27.480 --> 00:21:30.410
and historically, again, you
can see everything that's

00:21:30.410 --> 00:21:32.450
going on inside of BigQuery.

00:21:32.450 --> 00:21:37.280
So today we have available
jobs and reservations we use.

00:21:37.280 --> 00:21:40.000
We're going to be soon
launching streaming views.

00:21:40.000 --> 00:21:41.750
So if you're streaming
data into BigQuery,

00:21:41.750 --> 00:21:44.310
you can see what's happening
there-- error rates,

00:21:44.310 --> 00:21:46.040
bytes ingested,
things like that.

00:21:46.040 --> 00:21:48.510
We're also going to
provide storage views.

00:21:48.510 --> 00:21:51.710
So you can see how much data
is stored in each your column,

00:21:51.710 --> 00:21:53.480
each of your petition,
each of your table,

00:21:53.480 --> 00:21:54.525
and how it's being used.

00:21:54.525 --> 00:21:56.150
And eventually, we're
going to actually

00:21:56.150 --> 00:21:58.140
get into permissions, as well.

00:21:58.140 --> 00:22:00.140
So some of the benefits
of information schema

00:22:00.140 --> 00:22:01.190
that we provide--

00:22:01.190 --> 00:22:04.010
well, for one, it's just
automatically managed by us.

00:22:04.010 --> 00:22:06.650
It's the data that
we provide for you.

00:22:06.650 --> 00:22:09.133
And we don't charge
you for the storage.

00:22:09.133 --> 00:22:10.050
So that's really nice.

00:22:10.050 --> 00:22:10.990
It's just there.

00:22:10.990 --> 00:22:12.530
and you can start using it.

00:22:12.530 --> 00:22:13.670
It's live, right?

00:22:13.670 --> 00:22:18.260
So this is consistent sub-second
latency of information

00:22:18.260 --> 00:22:19.520
that's provided to you.

00:22:19.520 --> 00:22:20.750
It's also historical.

00:22:20.750 --> 00:22:23.780
So we get up to six months
of historical information

00:22:23.780 --> 00:22:27.030
in these tables,
automatically free of charge.

00:22:27.030 --> 00:22:29.270
The other benefit is,
for larger organizations

00:22:29.270 --> 00:22:31.280
that have lots and
lots of projects,

00:22:31.280 --> 00:22:33.770
we provide you an
organization-wide view.

00:22:33.770 --> 00:22:36.980
So you don't have to
query every single project

00:22:36.980 --> 00:22:38.330
to figure out what's going on.

00:22:38.330 --> 00:22:40.250
You can query
information schema tables

00:22:40.250 --> 00:22:42.700
for your entire organization.

00:22:42.700 --> 00:22:44.940
So there's lots of really
interesting use cases

00:22:44.940 --> 00:22:46.870
that you can leverage
these tables with.

00:22:49.650 --> 00:22:52.098
Fast forward a few
months, and 23andMeow has

00:22:52.098 --> 00:22:53.390
been really, really successful.

00:22:53.390 --> 00:22:56.160
But we've decided to
launch some new features.

00:22:56.160 --> 00:22:58.440
Because we have
been capturing sound

00:22:58.440 --> 00:23:02.617
for a long time using our
microphone on these collars,

00:23:02.617 --> 00:23:04.200
we started developing
machine learning

00:23:04.200 --> 00:23:07.950
models that help us understand
what the cat is actually

00:23:07.950 --> 00:23:08.910
saying.

00:23:08.910 --> 00:23:11.340
And then we've developed
a two-way Language

00:23:11.340 --> 00:23:13.950
API that allows us
to understand cats,

00:23:13.950 --> 00:23:15.870
but also to communicate back.

00:23:15.870 --> 00:23:19.680
So it will actually translate
human English language--

00:23:19.680 --> 00:23:21.600
only English is
supported, for now--

00:23:21.600 --> 00:23:23.790
into cat meows and purrs.

00:23:23.790 --> 00:23:26.400
So it allows us to communicate
directly with the cat.

00:23:26.400 --> 00:23:29.100
This is a fantastic
feature, and it immediately

00:23:29.100 --> 00:23:31.980
propelled us to become
a global phenomena.

00:23:31.980 --> 00:23:35.275
We have, today, a billion
daily active cats.

00:23:35.275 --> 00:23:36.900
And we're a multinational
organization.

00:23:36.900 --> 00:23:38.820
We operate in 145 countries.

00:23:38.820 --> 00:23:42.360
It's just amazing success
that we've experienced so far.

00:23:42.360 --> 00:23:45.340
But we've grown so fast.

00:23:45.340 --> 00:23:48.330
And we are a larger
organization, now.

00:23:48.330 --> 00:23:50.460
But we're still using
BigQuery is the same core

00:23:50.460 --> 00:23:51.640
of our technology.

00:23:51.640 --> 00:23:54.390
So how do we obtain
more control?

00:23:54.390 --> 00:23:56.730
What kinds of problems are
we facing as a billion daily

00:23:56.730 --> 00:23:59.080
active cat organization?

00:23:59.080 --> 00:24:01.170
So, for one, is we
really care about price.

00:24:01.170 --> 00:24:04.050
We're about to go
IPO, so we need

00:24:04.050 --> 00:24:06.090
to get our finances in order.

00:24:06.090 --> 00:24:07.610
So we need to control the cost.

00:24:07.610 --> 00:24:10.110
We need to control the cost of
the organization as a whole,

00:24:10.110 --> 00:24:12.090
and we need to control
per-organization

00:24:12.090 --> 00:24:14.860
all the way down to
individual users.

00:24:14.860 --> 00:24:17.520
We also have some
really critical SLAs.

00:24:17.520 --> 00:24:20.340
We actually run the machine
learning models often right

00:24:20.340 --> 00:24:21.360
in BigQuery.

00:24:21.360 --> 00:24:23.017
So those are very
important workflows

00:24:23.017 --> 00:24:24.600
that we have, and
we need to make sure

00:24:24.600 --> 00:24:26.520
that those get the
right resources

00:24:26.520 --> 00:24:28.395
and they finish in the
right amounts of time.

00:24:28.395 --> 00:24:30.603
So that's critical for us
to have workload management

00:24:30.603 --> 00:24:31.300
capabilities.

00:24:31.300 --> 00:24:33.140
And the third one
is cost attribution.

00:24:33.140 --> 00:24:35.970
Again, we should be able to
trace every single action

00:24:35.970 --> 00:24:39.210
that's happening in our
environment, down to the user

00:24:39.210 --> 00:24:42.920
that has caused that action,
so that we can attribute costs.

00:24:42.920 --> 00:24:46.020
So these are all very much
enterprise-grade billion

00:24:46.020 --> 00:24:49.450
daily active cat problems
that we're facing.

00:24:49.450 --> 00:24:51.050
So to support
these use cases, we

00:24:51.050 --> 00:24:52.720
launched BigQuery Reservations.

00:24:52.720 --> 00:24:54.930
This is a general
availability platform

00:24:54.930 --> 00:24:59.070
for workflow management and cost
management inside of BigQuery.

00:24:59.070 --> 00:25:01.080
BigQuery Reservations
today enables

00:25:01.080 --> 00:25:03.660
you to purchase slot commitments
in seconds-- again, monthly,

00:25:03.660 --> 00:25:05.730
annual, and flex types.

00:25:05.730 --> 00:25:11.310
Then you can take that capacity,
and you can compartmentalize.

00:25:11.310 --> 00:25:14.520
You can isolate and partition
it into individual departments

00:25:14.520 --> 00:25:17.070
or teams or sets of users.

00:25:17.070 --> 00:25:19.440
So if you have x
amount of capacity,

00:25:19.440 --> 00:25:23.400
you want to give the individual
use cases their isolated pools.

00:25:23.400 --> 00:25:25.360
So we allow you to do that.

00:25:25.360 --> 00:25:28.860
And finally, it allows you
to share any unused capacity

00:25:28.860 --> 00:25:30.310
across your organization.

00:25:30.310 --> 00:25:32.610
So in the periods when
there's not a lot going on,

00:25:32.610 --> 00:25:34.030
that capacity is not wasted.

00:25:34.030 --> 00:25:36.030
It's available for the
rest of your organization

00:25:36.030 --> 00:25:40.180
to be used seamlessly
and automatically.

00:25:40.180 --> 00:25:41.990
So let's think about
a typical use case--

00:25:41.990 --> 00:25:44.740
so how reservations
are used in practice.

00:25:44.740 --> 00:25:48.480
So let's say we've figured
out that 1,000 slots is what

00:25:48.480 --> 00:25:50.220
we need in terms of capacity.

00:25:50.220 --> 00:25:52.380
And we said that
500 of those slots

00:25:52.380 --> 00:25:54.560
are going to be our
steady state slots,

00:25:54.560 --> 00:25:57.000
and we can lock them in
on a very inexpensive

00:25:57.000 --> 00:25:58.470
annual contract.

00:25:58.470 --> 00:26:01.140
And then 500 slots are
going to be flex slots,

00:26:01.140 --> 00:26:03.330
simply because maybe we
want to have more or less.

00:26:03.330 --> 00:26:04.480
We're not entirely sure.

00:26:04.480 --> 00:26:06.060
So we do want that flexibility.

00:26:06.060 --> 00:26:08.220
But in total, we
have 1,000 slots.

00:26:08.220 --> 00:26:11.650
That's our capacity
for data processing.

00:26:11.650 --> 00:26:13.260
A slot is a slot.

00:26:13.260 --> 00:26:16.110
Step number two, we're going
to take those 1,000 slots

00:26:16.110 --> 00:26:18.490
and we have three
different business units.

00:26:18.490 --> 00:26:21.960
We have the team that
manages Pheromones API.

00:26:21.960 --> 00:26:24.480
We have a team that
manages the Languages API.

00:26:24.480 --> 00:26:27.590
And we have a team that
manages BI, all the dashboard.

00:26:27.590 --> 00:26:29.950
That's three separate teams
within our organization.

00:26:29.950 --> 00:26:31.920
And the pheromones
is, of course,

00:26:31.920 --> 00:26:33.690
where we make all of our money.

00:26:33.690 --> 00:26:36.110
So we're going to
give them 500 slots.

00:26:36.110 --> 00:26:37.878
The languages team
is really important,

00:26:37.878 --> 00:26:38.920
but it's a newer feature.

00:26:38.920 --> 00:26:40.830
So we would give them 300 slots.

00:26:40.830 --> 00:26:43.050
And then BI is going
to get 200 slots.

00:26:43.050 --> 00:26:44.730
So we've partitioned
these workloads

00:26:44.730 --> 00:26:46.860
into these departments.

00:26:46.860 --> 00:26:48.480
And finally, the
third step is, we

00:26:48.480 --> 00:26:52.470
assigned all the relevant
projects to these reservations.

00:26:52.470 --> 00:26:53.640
And off we go.

00:26:53.640 --> 00:26:56.715
That's all we have to
do-- set and forget.

00:26:56.715 --> 00:26:58.090
Here's a new
feature that's going

00:26:58.090 --> 00:27:01.060
to help even further with
this type of use case.

00:27:01.060 --> 00:27:04.300
BigQuery Slots Autoscaling
is coming soon.

00:27:04.300 --> 00:27:06.490
What BigQuery Slots
Autoscaling does

00:27:06.490 --> 00:27:10.150
is it automatically provisions
and deprovisions capacity

00:27:10.150 --> 00:27:13.880
for you, based on how
you're using our platform.

00:27:13.880 --> 00:27:16.310
So it's really, really
advanced functionality.

00:27:16.310 --> 00:27:19.240
So again, because we're not
running on virtual machines,

00:27:19.240 --> 00:27:22.685
because we are serverless,
we're able to give you a lot

00:27:22.685 --> 00:27:25.060
of benefits that a traditional
virtual machine-based data

00:27:25.060 --> 00:27:26.480
warehouse cannot.

00:27:26.480 --> 00:27:29.710
For one is we had a
zero second warm-up.

00:27:29.710 --> 00:27:32.860
The moment you get your
capacity provisioned,

00:27:32.860 --> 00:27:35.470
your capacity is at
100% performance.

00:27:35.470 --> 00:27:37.870
There's no such thing as
hydrating your local disk

00:27:37.870 --> 00:27:40.180
to make sure that
performance is optimal.

00:27:40.180 --> 00:27:43.360
Second, as I already mentioned,
whenever you increase capacity,

00:27:43.360 --> 00:27:46.450
your queries in flight take
advantage of that immediately.

00:27:46.450 --> 00:27:49.480
The third is, again, because
we're not running local disk,

00:27:49.480 --> 00:27:51.640
because we're not
relying on local storage,

00:27:51.640 --> 00:27:53.500
there are no
unexpected behaviors,

00:27:53.500 --> 00:27:56.590
like performance clips when the
local storage goes away or gets

00:27:56.590 --> 00:27:58.000
saturated.

00:27:58.000 --> 00:27:59.350
We don't rely on local storage.

00:27:59.350 --> 00:28:02.200
We have true separation
of storage and compute.

00:28:02.200 --> 00:28:05.680
And finally, we express
this in our SLA.

00:28:05.680 --> 00:28:09.250
We're immune to downtime for
individual nodes, all the way

00:28:09.250 --> 00:28:11.390
to large portions
of our data center.

00:28:11.390 --> 00:28:13.930
And that's why we're able to
provide four nines of the SLA.

00:28:13.930 --> 00:28:17.930
So let's talk about Slots
Autoscaling a little bit more.

00:28:17.930 --> 00:28:21.940
So all we're doing here is we're
adding one additional variable

00:28:21.940 --> 00:28:24.190
to the Reservations
configuration.

00:28:24.190 --> 00:28:27.760
It's called max
autoscaling limit.

00:28:27.760 --> 00:28:29.710
So let's walk
through this example.

00:28:29.710 --> 00:28:32.290
So what we did is, for the
pheromones reservation,

00:28:32.290 --> 00:28:35.710
we set the autoscaling
limit to be 300.

00:28:35.710 --> 00:28:39.040
And for the BI reservation,
we set the autoscaling limit

00:28:39.040 --> 00:28:40.300
to be 1,000.

00:28:40.300 --> 00:28:42.020
And so we did this
per reservation.

00:28:42.020 --> 00:28:43.780
That's where this
configuration lives.

00:28:43.780 --> 00:28:47.140
But we didn't enable this
on the language reservation.

00:28:47.140 --> 00:28:50.230
And so what happens is,
whenever the pheromones

00:28:50.230 --> 00:28:53.790
reservation needs
more than 500 slots,

00:28:53.790 --> 00:28:56.680
it will try to get that 500
slots from across the rest

00:28:56.680 --> 00:28:57.790
of the organization.

00:28:57.790 --> 00:29:00.650
It will try to see if the
languages reservation has

00:29:00.650 --> 00:29:04.930
idle capacity or if BI
reservation has idle capacity.

00:29:04.930 --> 00:29:06.850
And then it's
going to say, well,

00:29:06.850 --> 00:29:08.590
I still need more
capacity than that

00:29:08.590 --> 00:29:12.070
because I have a lot of
data processing to do.

00:29:12.070 --> 00:29:15.810
And only then will reservations
autoscale or kick in.

00:29:15.810 --> 00:29:17.890
Only then we will
increase our capacity

00:29:17.890 --> 00:29:21.010
by 300 slots,
which is fantastic.

00:29:21.010 --> 00:29:23.350
So the only way
auto-scaling and kicks

00:29:23.350 --> 00:29:26.720
in is if the entire
organization is saturated

00:29:26.720 --> 00:29:29.470
and there are no
idle slots to be had.

00:29:29.470 --> 00:29:32.590
And then, if the capacity
is no longer necessary,

00:29:32.590 --> 00:29:35.200
we will automatically
downscale your reservation.

00:29:35.200 --> 00:29:38.500
So pheromones will go from
500 slots to 800 slots

00:29:38.500 --> 00:29:42.880
and down, whereas BI will
go from 200 to 1,200 slots

00:29:42.880 --> 00:29:43.905
and down.

00:29:43.905 --> 00:29:45.340
Now, something I
should mention is

00:29:45.340 --> 00:29:48.600
that, the current
version of autoscaling

00:29:48.600 --> 00:29:51.460
that we're working
on is binary, which

00:29:51.460 --> 00:29:54.760
means that we will only
increase to the maximum amount

00:29:54.760 --> 00:29:56.680
or back to the minimum amount.

00:29:56.680 --> 00:29:58.690
So in this example, the
pheromones reservation

00:29:58.690 --> 00:30:02.260
will only be at size
500 or size 800.

00:30:02.260 --> 00:30:03.765
There's no intermediate state.

00:30:03.765 --> 00:30:05.140
And of course,
eventually we will

00:30:05.140 --> 00:30:10.380
have all these advanced
autoscaling modes, as well.

00:30:10.380 --> 00:30:14.420
So how does autoscaling work
with steady state capacity?

00:30:14.420 --> 00:30:16.140
Well, really, you
think about it--

00:30:16.140 --> 00:30:18.380
you can purchase monthly
annual flex commitments

00:30:18.380 --> 00:30:22.330
and keep them as steady state
capacity that is always there,

00:30:22.330 --> 00:30:23.770
that is guaranteed for you.

00:30:23.770 --> 00:30:25.340
And then you can
turn on autoscaling

00:30:25.340 --> 00:30:28.880
on top of that to provide
burst, and it's managed for you.

00:30:28.880 --> 00:30:32.660
Autoscaling only kicks
in when necessary.

00:30:32.660 --> 00:30:33.890
How do you pay for it?

00:30:33.890 --> 00:30:37.250
Well, what we do is we
purchase flex slots for you.

00:30:37.250 --> 00:30:41.130
So autoscaling underneath
the hood uses flex slots.

00:30:41.130 --> 00:30:44.490
And so you play the
flex slots rates.

00:30:44.490 --> 00:30:47.570
Now, here's a really,
really advanced capability

00:30:47.570 --> 00:30:51.260
that autoscaling provides
for you, starting from zero.

00:30:51.260 --> 00:30:55.380
This is called the "auto-pause"
or "auto-start" use case.

00:30:55.380 --> 00:30:58.310
So in this example, we
actually have zero commitments.

00:30:58.310 --> 00:31:02.340
We've purchased zero slots,
and all of our reservations

00:31:02.340 --> 00:31:05.060
are sized to be zero, which
means that there's not

00:31:05.060 --> 00:31:07.310
a single slot purchased
or provisioned

00:31:07.310 --> 00:31:10.550
and you pay us nothing in
terms of analytic capability.

00:31:10.550 --> 00:31:13.200
But we set autoscaling
for each reservation.

00:31:13.200 --> 00:31:15.020
So pheromones,
language, BI, they all

00:31:15.020 --> 00:31:17.360
have autoscaling
with limits set.

00:31:17.360 --> 00:31:21.060
In this mode, whenever a query
comes into the environment,

00:31:21.060 --> 00:31:23.510
we will immediately
scale the reservation

00:31:23.510 --> 00:31:27.500
to its maximum limit, and the
query will execute immediately.

00:31:27.500 --> 00:31:30.425
And then, on the opposite
side, whenever your reservation

00:31:30.425 --> 00:31:34.010
is idle, we will
diminish it back to zero.

00:31:34.010 --> 00:31:37.040
So this is essentially
auto-pause, auto-start.

00:31:37.040 --> 00:31:38.810
Your BigQuery
environment will only

00:31:38.810 --> 00:31:40.730
operate when you're
running queries,

00:31:40.730 --> 00:31:42.330
and it will shut
down when you queries

00:31:42.330 --> 00:31:44.000
are not running,
which will enable you

00:31:44.000 --> 00:31:45.292
to save a good amount of money.

00:31:49.102 --> 00:31:50.810
So I've talked about
a number of features

00:31:50.810 --> 00:31:52.550
that enable you to
kind of traverse

00:31:52.550 --> 00:31:54.140
the gradient of growth--

00:31:54.140 --> 00:31:56.150
from a tiny little
start up, or just trying

00:31:56.150 --> 00:31:59.600
to go to market quickly, to
a midsize organization, all

00:31:59.600 --> 00:32:01.550
the way to a
multinational organization

00:32:01.550 --> 00:32:05.400
with complex requirements and
complex needs and reporting

00:32:05.400 --> 00:32:06.660
and so on and so forth.

00:32:06.660 --> 00:32:08.350
So let's kind of
summarize everything

00:32:08.350 --> 00:32:09.350
that we've talked about.

00:32:09.350 --> 00:32:12.080
So on the pricing front,
we have flex slots

00:32:12.080 --> 00:32:14.640
that went GA at the of June.

00:32:14.640 --> 00:32:16.820
We recently launched 100 slots.

00:32:16.820 --> 00:32:22.040
So now you can purchase capacity
for as little as $2,000 a month

00:32:22.040 --> 00:32:25.490
with a monthly
contract, or $4 per hour

00:32:25.490 --> 00:32:26.982
with the flex contract.

00:32:26.982 --> 00:32:28.940
And the other thing we
talked about quite a bit

00:32:28.940 --> 00:32:30.580
is the fact that we
share idle capacity

00:32:30.580 --> 00:32:31.580
across your environment.

00:32:31.580 --> 00:32:34.040
It doesn't go to waste.

00:32:34.040 --> 00:32:37.100
The second point is workload
management capability.

00:32:37.100 --> 00:32:39.380
So once you have
this capacity, how

00:32:39.380 --> 00:32:42.870
do you actually break it up into
your department's workloads?

00:32:42.870 --> 00:32:46.070
Well, Reservations is a powerful
platform that enables that,

00:32:46.070 --> 00:32:47.840
and it's GA.

00:32:47.840 --> 00:32:50.300
But we recently announced
Slots Autoscaling

00:32:50.300 --> 00:32:52.790
with that additional
level of automation

00:32:52.790 --> 00:32:54.460
that enables you to
scale up and down

00:32:54.460 --> 00:32:56.510
and only pay for
what you consume.

00:32:56.510 --> 00:32:58.470
And finally, we talked
about Admin Panel,

00:32:58.470 --> 00:33:00.980
which is the visual UI
that we will provide

00:33:00.980 --> 00:33:03.110
to help you monitor
and perform root cause

00:33:03.110 --> 00:33:06.280
analysis on your environment.

00:33:06.280 --> 00:33:09.120
And finally, intelligence--
higher level data

00:33:09.120 --> 00:33:12.750
and logic it will provide
to you that will help you

00:33:12.750 --> 00:33:14.140
drive your decision making.

00:33:14.140 --> 00:33:17.160
So of course, information
schema is a project

00:33:17.160 --> 00:33:18.870
that will have a
series of views that

00:33:18.870 --> 00:33:21.900
will enable you to get data
and slot recommendations-- just

00:33:21.900 --> 00:33:25.580
a first step in this advanced
intelligence that will tell you

00:33:25.580 --> 00:33:27.330
exactly what's going
on in the environment

00:33:27.330 --> 00:33:29.120
and will recommend to
you further action.

00:33:31.950 --> 00:33:35.880
Now, actual administration
and performance

00:33:35.880 --> 00:33:37.950
is just one part of the story.

00:33:37.950 --> 00:33:40.110
Of course, as an
administrator, oftentimes, you

00:33:40.110 --> 00:33:41.350
care about performance.

00:33:41.350 --> 00:33:43.350
We'll continue to
invest in this area.

00:33:43.350 --> 00:33:46.050
Throughout the years we've
always BigQuery faster, faster,

00:33:46.050 --> 00:33:47.710
faster, and faster.

00:33:47.710 --> 00:33:50.220
So just from the beginning
of this year, alone--

00:33:50.220 --> 00:33:51.720
from January 1--

00:33:51.720 --> 00:33:54.960
we look at the industry
standard TPC-DS benchmarks,

00:33:54.960 --> 00:33:57.600
and we measure our
performance improvements

00:33:57.600 --> 00:34:01.030
to be anywhere from 20% to 30%.

00:34:01.030 --> 00:34:02.880
And the way you get
this benefit is just

00:34:02.880 --> 00:34:04.860
the next time you run
a query, you will just

00:34:04.860 --> 00:34:06.820
get better performance.

00:34:06.820 --> 00:34:10.530
We perform these optimizations
for you on the background.

00:34:10.530 --> 00:34:12.918
We roll out code without
disturbing your environment.

00:34:12.918 --> 00:34:15.210
You just get these benefits,
and you get these benefits

00:34:15.210 --> 00:34:17.020
without any additional cost.

00:34:17.020 --> 00:34:18.840
So this is a trend that we have.

00:34:18.840 --> 00:34:22.320
Every few months we add
some better performance

00:34:22.320 --> 00:34:23.060
optimizations.

00:34:23.060 --> 00:34:24.810
Your queries just
become faster and faster

00:34:24.810 --> 00:34:25.929
and faster and faster.

00:34:25.929 --> 00:34:29.130
And this trend will
continue in the future.

00:34:29.130 --> 00:34:31.139
So we talked about
this quite a bit.

00:34:31.139 --> 00:34:33.090
When you're starting
out, you really

00:34:33.090 --> 00:34:35.370
want the easiest
path to go to market.

00:34:35.370 --> 00:34:37.210
You don't want to
worry about things.

00:34:37.210 --> 00:34:39.690
And so our serverless capability
enables you to do that.

00:34:39.690 --> 00:34:41.010
There's nothing to set up.

00:34:41.010 --> 00:34:43.710
You only pay for what you
consume, and off you go.

00:34:43.710 --> 00:34:46.199
But as you grow, you care
more about different things.

00:34:46.199 --> 00:34:48.620
You care more about
control and predictability.

00:34:48.620 --> 00:34:51.239
So we've built out a series
of capabilities that enable

00:34:51.239 --> 00:34:52.690
you to take advantage of that.

00:34:52.690 --> 00:34:56.500
But the key is that all of
these things are optional.

00:34:56.500 --> 00:34:58.500
You only need to use
Admin Panel if you

00:34:58.500 --> 00:35:00.810
care about predictability
and control

00:35:00.810 --> 00:35:02.350
and you're a certain size.

00:35:02.350 --> 00:35:04.890
So please be mindful
of that, because we

00:35:04.890 --> 00:35:08.007
have a lot of customers
that are on the larger side,

00:35:08.007 --> 00:35:09.590
but we have lots and
lots of customers

00:35:09.590 --> 00:35:13.170
that are on the free tier or
have very, very little data

00:35:13.170 --> 00:35:14.070
inside of BigQuery.

00:35:14.070 --> 00:35:15.650
And that's perfectly OK.

00:35:15.650 --> 00:35:18.590
BigQuery is built for everyone.

00:35:18.590 --> 00:35:20.640
We recently talked
about trial slots.

00:35:20.640 --> 00:35:23.360
So this is a promotion
for new BigQuery customers

00:35:23.360 --> 00:35:27.380
that enables you to
get 95% off of slots.

00:35:27.380 --> 00:35:30.800
So essentially, you
can buy 500 slots

00:35:30.800 --> 00:35:34.410
for a duration of six
months for $500 per month.

00:35:34.410 --> 00:35:37.920
This is 1/20th of the cost
of a monthly contract.

00:35:37.920 --> 00:35:41.425
So it's just $1
per slot per month.

00:35:41.425 --> 00:35:43.550
Of course, it's just slots,
so you can combine them

00:35:43.550 --> 00:35:45.530
with other types of slots.

00:35:45.530 --> 00:35:48.620
You can combine them
with other flex slots--

00:35:48.620 --> 00:35:50.270
monthly, annually, and so forth.

00:35:50.270 --> 00:35:53.450
And of course, it covers all of
your SQL queries that you run.

00:35:53.450 --> 00:35:55.440
So it's a good
amount of capacity.

00:35:55.440 --> 00:35:58.850
Now, I should be mindful to tell
you that this is a promotion,

00:35:58.850 --> 00:36:03.900
so certain qualifications
and restrictions do apply.

00:36:03.900 --> 00:36:06.960
So finally, I want to
open the floor to everyone

00:36:06.960 --> 00:36:09.870
to think about the
next 10x use case

00:36:09.870 --> 00:36:12.390
for our tiny little
startup, 23andMeow.

00:36:12.390 --> 00:36:14.460
There's lots of things
that cats can do,

00:36:14.460 --> 00:36:16.440
and we're open to collaboration.

00:36:16.440 --> 00:36:17.890
We're open to more ideas.

00:36:17.890 --> 00:36:22.080
So send me some of your ideas
on Twitter to the Tino team.

00:36:22.080 --> 00:36:23.950
And I really
appreciate all the time

00:36:23.950 --> 00:36:25.270
that you spent listening to me.

00:36:25.270 --> 00:36:27.080
Thank you so much.

